
/* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Desktop program (D4) — APPEND-ONLY SECTION. Everything above this line is unchanged.
   1. Self-hosted fonts   2. Shell layout offset   3. Title strip   4. Banner / notices   5. Boot gate
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* 1. Self-hosted fonts: ui/public/fonts (SIL OFL 1.1, provenance in ui/public/fonts/README.md). The exact
   Google Fonts files the terminal used before (Chakra Petch v13 static 500/600/700, JetBrains Mono v24
   variable), latin + latin-ext subsets, same unicode-range split, font-display: swap. No request leaves the
   machine. JetBrains Mono has no rupee sign (U+20B9, checked in the font's cmap): the last four faces give the
   'JetBrains Mono' family a rupee from Chakra Petch (which has one), weight-matched, so every "₹" in the mono
   UI renders identically in WebKit and Chromium instead of whatever system fallback each engine picks.
   Later rules win for overlapping ranges, so the rupee faces are declared last. */
@font-face { font-family: 'Chakra Petch'; font-style: normal; font-weight: 500; font-display: swap;
  src: url('/fonts/chakra-petch-v13-500-latin-ext.woff2') format('woff2');
  unicode-range: U+0100-02BA, U+02BD-02C5, U+02C7-02CC, U+02CE-02D7, U+02DD-02FF, U+0304, U+0308, U+0329, U+1D00-1DBF, U+1E00-1E9F, U+1EF2-1EFF, U+2020, U+20A0-20AB, U+20AD-20C4, U+2113, U+2C60-2C7F, U+A720-A7FF; }
@font-face { font-family: 'Chakra Petch'; font-style: normal; font-weight: 500; font-display: swap;
  src: url('/fonts/chakra-petch-v13-500-latin.woff2') format('woff2');
  unicode-range: U+0000-00FF, U+0131, U+0152-0153, U+02BB-02BC, U+02C6, U+02DA, U+02DC, U+0304, U+0308, U+0329, U+2000-206F, U+20AC, U+2122, U+2191, U+2193, U+2212, U+2215, U+FEFF, U+FFFD; }
